I find it interesting to keep in touch with the diagnostics interface of =
cars over the ODB2 connector.

1) Consider youtube video "Exploring a cheap OBD2 vehicle diagnostics =
module" (https://youtu.be/HrL6fDFt-Rs )?

	=3D=3D> At around 1:43 it seems he shows and describe the ODB2 =
connector nicely (https://youtu.be/HrL6fDFt-Rs?t=3D103 )?

=EF=BF=BC

2) Consider the CAN bus protocol as defined by ISO 15765-4 =
(https://www.iso.org/standard/78384.html )

"This document specifies requirements for CAN-based communication =
systems between the in-vehicle network and the diagnostic link connector =
of the vehicle. "

	=3D=3D> Also see Wikipedia "CAN Bus" =
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAN_bus )

"A controller area network (CAN) is a vehicle bus standard designed to =
enable efficient communication primarily between electronic control =
units (ECUs). Originally developed to reduce the complexity and cost of =
electrical wiring in automobiles through multiplexing, the CAN bus =
protocol has since been adopted in various other contexts, such as 3D =
printing[1]."

3) Consider the ODB2 connector as defined by ISO 9141 =
(https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/en/#iso:std:iso:9141:ed-1:v1:en )

	=3D=3D> Also see Wikipedia "On-board diagnostics" =
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On-board_diagnostics )

	=3D=3D> It seems 'big clive' has used the wikipedia available =
coloured view of the ODB2 connector pins?

=EF=BF=BC
=
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:OBD-II_type_A_female_connector_pinout.=
svg )

4) Consider SAE J1850 PWM and VPW is part of ODB2 =
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On-board_diagnostics#SAE_standards_document=
s_on_OBD-II )

Standards documents
SAE standards documents on OBD-II
J1962 =E2=80=93 Defines the physical connector used for the OBD-II =
interface.
J1850 =E2=80=93 Defines a serial data protocol. There are two variants: =
10.4 kbit/s (single wire, VPW) and 41.6 kbit/s (2 wire, PWM). Mainly =
used by US manufacturers, also known as PCI (Chrysler, 10.4K), Class 2 =
(GM, 10.4K), and SCP (Ford, 41.6K)
J1978 =E2=80=93 Defines minimal operating standards for OBD-II scan =
tools
J1979 =E2=80=93 Defines standards for diagnostic test modes
J2012 =E2=80=93 Defines standards trouble codes and definitions.
J2178-1 =E2=80=93 Defines standards for network message header formats =
and physical address assignments
J2178-2 =E2=80=93 Gives data parameter definitions
J2178-3 =E2=80=93 Defines standards for network message frame IDs for =
single byte headers
J2178-4 =E2=80=93 Defines standards for network messages with three byte =
headers
J2284-3 =E2=80=93 Defines 500K CAN physical and data link layer
J2411 =E2=80=93 Describes the GMLAN (Single-Wire CAN) protocol, used in =
newer GM vehicles. Often accessible on the OBD connector as PIN 1 on =
newer GM vehicles

...=

